    <briefdescription>
<para>A class for describing the cylindrical 3D mapping. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>The mapping <formula id="85">$ (R, Z, \zeta)\mapsto (x,y,z) $</formula> is defined as follow :</para>
<para><formula id="86">$ x(R, Z, \zeta) = R \cos(\zeta), $</formula></para>
<para><formula id="87">$ y(R, Z, \zeta) = R \sin(\zeta). $</formula></para>
<para><formula id="88">$ z(R, Z, \zeta) = z. $</formula></para>
<para>It and its Jacobian matrix are invertible everywhere except for <formula id="39">$ R = 0 $</formula>.</para>
<para>The Jacobian matrix coefficients are defined as follow</para>
<para><formula id="89">$ J^x_{\;R}(R, Z, \zeta) = \cos(\zeta) $</formula></para>
<para><formula id="90">$ J^x_{\;Z}(R, Z, \zeta) = 0 $</formula></para>
<para><formula id="91">$ J^x_{\;\zeta}(R, Z, \zeta) = - R \sin(\zeta) $</formula></para>
<para><formula id="92">$ J^y_{\;R}(R, Z, \zeta) = \sin(\zeta) $</formula></para>
<para><formula id="93">$ J^y_{\;Z}(R, Z, \zeta) = 0 $</formula></para>
<para><formula id="94">$ J^y_{\;\zeta}(R, Z, \zeta) = R \cos(\zeta) $</formula></para>
<para><formula id="95">$ J^z_{\;R}(R, Z, \zeta) = 0 $</formula></para>
<para><formula id="96">$ J^z_{\;Z}(R, Z, \zeta) = 1 $</formula></para>
<para><formula id="97">$ J^z_{\;\zeta}(R, Z, \zeta) = 0 $</formula></para>
<para>and the matrix determinant: <formula id="98">$ \det(J) = -R $</formula>. </para>
    </detaileddescription>
